


@@BEGIN: PRESENTATION
@id: rohrmeier
@title: Musicology at Scale: From Expert-Annotated Corpora to Computational Models of Music and its Evolution
@presenter: rohrmeier
@abstract: 

Musicology has long navigated fundamental methodological
tensions: while qualitative close readings of canonical
masterworks bring profound interpretive insights, verifying
broad macro-historical narratives across decades and
centuries faces fundamental challenges of reproducibility,
scalability, and verifiability. How can we methodologically
bridge an individual composer's stylistic innovation with
overarching systemic change? How can we adopt and leverage
current computational methods to explore large musical
datasets and enrich our understanding of music and its
historical development?

This keynote presents an overview of long-term research
developed at the Digital and Cognitive Musicology Lab
(DCML), showing our path from the foundational labor of
corpus building to quantitative models of musical structure
and diachronic evolution.

<h3>The Epistemological Challenge: Scalability, Verifiability, and Corpus Criticism</h3>

While the field previously suffered from lack of digital
data, collaborative initiatives over the past fifteen years
have substantially changed the situation. Creating
large-scale symbolic corpora requires the formulation of
explicit annotation standards, rigorous data engineering,
extensive annotation and curation effort as well as
long-term financial stability. Corpora, however, are never
neutral: they inherit canon biases, survival skews, and
institutional filters which demand detailed criticism.
Thus, methodological transparency and reflection are
central prerequisites for turning empirical datasets into
the foundation for digital musicological research.

<h3>The Role of the Model: "Choose Your Grammar"</h3>

Contrary to a pervasive methodological misconception, there
is no such thing as theory-free or theory-neutral data
exploration. Every computational representation, from basic
Markov models and n-gram transitions to tree-rewriting
rules and beyond, carries implicit assumptions about a
specific music-theoretical ontology and formal grammar.
Digital corpus research does not eliminate theoretical
bias; rather, it forces our music-theoretical assumptions
to become overt, mathematically explicit, transparent, and
contestable.

<h3>Data-Driven Explorations: Distributions, Tonal Spaces, and
Harmonic Trajectories<h3>

Leveraging curated corpora and theoretical models enables
us to explore three centuries of music in new ways. Drawing
on our recent work, we illustrate the power of simple and
advanced statistics of pitch and harmony. From another
angle, adopting different formal representations of tonal
spaces into empirical research opens a new avenue to trace
diachronic evolution. Finally, expert-annotated harmonic
labels make it possible to chart large-scale harmonic
trajectories, trace regime shifts and identify
statistically exceptional individual compositions.

In general, quantitative methods and computational modeling
do not seek to supplant traditional historical
interpretation. Instead, they provide a range of different
pathways and lenses, allowing music researchers to
critically evaluate, ground and extend musical analyses and
historical narratives within contestable empirical
evidence.

@@END: PRESENTATION



